An authentic homemade tribute to the beloved Cracker Barrel roadside restaurant favorite. Freshly grated russet potatoes are thoroughly drained for maximum crispiness, then tossed with Colby cheese, beaten eggs, garlic powder, and sour cream before baking into a cheesy, golden casserole topped with crumbled bacon.

*Adapted from [Delish](https://www.delish.com/cooking/recipe-ideas/a38108810/cracker-barrel-hashbrown-casserole-copycat-recipe/).*

## Ingredients

- 4 to 5 large russet potatoes (approx. 2 ½ lbs), peeled
- ½ large yellow onion, peeled
- 1 ¾ cups shredded Colby cheese (or mild cheddar), divided
- 3 large eggs, lightly beaten
- ¼ cup sour cream
- 2 teaspoons garlic powder
- 2 teaspoons kosher salt
- ½ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper
- Nonstick cooking spray or softened butter
- 2 thick-cut bacon strips, cooked crispy and crumbled

## Instructions

1. **Preheat and Prepare the Oven:** Position an oven rack in the middle slot and preheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Lightly grease a 2-to-3-quart baking dish (or a 10-to-12-inch cast iron skillet) with cooking spray or butter.
2. **Grate Potatoes and Onion:** Using the coarse holes of a box grater or the shredding disc of a food processor, shred the peeled russet potatoes and onion.
3. **Squeeze Out All Liquid:** Working in two or three batches, gather handfuls of the shredded potatoes and onion into a clean kitchen tea towel or multiple layers of cheesecloth. Twist and wring the bundle firmly over the sink or a bowl. Squeezing out this excess liquid and surface starch is the key step to prevent sogginess and ensure tender shreds that crisp up in the oven. Transfer the squeezed shreds to a large mixing bowl.
4. **Fold in Cheese and Seasonings:** Add 1 cup of the shredded Colby cheese, beaten eggs, sour cream, garlic powder, kosher salt, and black pepper into the bowl with the potatoes. Toss thoroughly with a spatula or your hands until every shred is lightly coated in the egg and sour cream mixture.
5. **Assemble in Dish:** Pour the potato-onion mixture into the prepared baking dish. Spread it evenly from edge to edge, avoiding pressing down too firmly so heat can circulate through the shreds.
6. **Add the Cheesy Bacon Crust:** Sprinkle the remaining ¾ cup of Colby cheese over the entire surface, then scatter the crumbled crispy bacon on top.
7. **Bake to a Golden Crisp:** Bake uncovered at 350°F for 55 to 65 minutes. The casserole is ready when the edges are richly browned and crisp, the cheese topping is golden and bubbling, and a fork slips easily through the center potatoes.
8. **Rest and Serve:** Allow the casserole to cool for 5 to 10 minutes on a trivet so the custard sets, making it easy to spoon and slice. Serve hot.

## Notes

- **Frozen Hashbrown Alternative:** You can substitute a 30 oz bag of frozen shredded hashbrowns. Thaw them completely in the refrigerator and pat thoroughly dry with paper towels before tossing with the freshly grated onion and other ingredients.
- **Cheese Variations:** While Colby cheese provides that classic mild diner flavor and creamy melt, Sharp Cheddar or Pepper Jack also work wonderfully.
